# DURABLE REPRO — a parenthesized BINOP key in a dict literal is misparsed as a
# CLOSURE param list: `{(a & b): 99}` becomes an array-of-closures value
# (`ClosureParam { type_: Named("a"), ownership: MutableBorrow, name: "b" }`),
# the unknown param type `a` never rejects, the array-of-closures into a
# `Dict[int, int]` binding never type-errors, and the program SIGBUSes at
# runtime (exit 135) on BOTH backends. `gg check` prints "OK: no semantic
# errors" — >=3 stacked bugs: the `looks_like_closure` shape heuristic
# (src/parser/expr.rs, patched 4x before, still holed); unknown-type closure
# params accepted; array-of-closures unified with Dict. The same hole opens for
# `^` the moment Caret leaves the deny-list — which is why D27 Round B's core
# is retiring this heuristic class, not deleting a match arm.
# Found by the R41 Round-B scout 2026-08-10; orchestrator-verified same day.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): `(a & b)` is a parenthesized bitwise-AND key
# expression -> {6 & 3 = 2: 99} -> prints 99.
int main():
    int a = 6
    int b = 3
    Dict[int, int] m = {(a & b): 99}
    print(m.get(2).unwrap())
    return 0
